<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rómulo Betancourt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rómulo Betancourt was a Venezuelan politician, founder of the social-democratic party Democratic Action, often called the father of Venezuelan democracy. He headed a revolutionary junta from 1945 to 1948 and, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcos-perez-jimenez">Marcos Pérez Jiménez</a>, was elected president for 1959–1964, defending democracy against coups and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>; his Betancourt Doctrine denied recognition to governments that seized power by force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romulo-betancourt.jpg" alt="Rómulo Betancourt" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Presidencia de la República de Venezuela · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foto_oficial_R%C3%B3mulo_Betancourt_1959.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
